---
import FamilyExplainerLayout from '../../layouts/FamilyExplainerLayout.astro';
import { Binary } from '@lucide/astro';

// Illustrative commands, verified against the asm198x CLI (2026-06-03).
// The full dialect/flag set lives on GitHub.
const snippets = [
  {
    label: 'Assemble C64 source (acme dialect)',
    code: 'asm198x --dialect acme countdown.s -o countdown.bin',
  },
  {
    label: 'Assemble + link a finished NES ROM (ca65)',
    code: 'asm198x --dialect ca65 game.asm -o game.nes',
  },
  {
    label: 'Disassemble a binary back to source',
    code: 'asm198x --disasm --dialect 6502 --org 0x0200 countdown.bin',
  },
];
---

<FamilyExplainerLayout
  name="Asm198x"
  title="Asm198x — the 198x family"
  description="Asm198x is one modern, single-binary assembler and disassembler for the retro CPUs of the era — source-compatible with the dialects people already use, validated byte-for-byte. Part of the 198x family."
  strapline="Modern assemblers & disassemblers for retro CPUs"
  status="New"
  icon={Binary}
  github="https://github.com/asm198x"
  note="Full docs, dialects, and the CPU roadmap live on GitHub."
>
    <p class="lead">
      Asm198x is the family's <a href="/family">assembler toolchain</a> — one modern,
      statically-linked, cross-platform binary that assembles <em>and</em> disassembles
      code for the retro CPUs of the era. Rather than invent a house syntax, each
      front-end matches a dialect people already use, and the output is checked{' '}
      <strong>byte-for-byte</strong> against that reference tool.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      The period assemblers are dead-OS binaries — you need DOSBox or a full emulator
      just to run them. The modern community tools mostly work, but they're fragmented:
      a different program, syntax and build dance per machine, much of it unmaintained
      C. Asm198x is one well-documented toolchain that spans the family's CPUs.
      <em>Rescue beats replace.</em>
    </p>

    <h2>What it covers</h2>
    <p>
      About twenty CPUs, in both directions: the 6502 family (including the 65816 and
      HuC6280), the Z80 family (Z80N, SM83, 8080, Z8000), the 6800, 6809 and 68000,
      and less common chips such as the CDP1802, Signetics 2650, PDP-11, TMS9900 and
      CP1610. The front-ends the curriculum depends on are each checked byte-identical
      against their reference assembler: <strong>6502</strong> (acme for the C64, ca65
      for the NES — with a bounded linker that emits a real <code>.nes</code> ROM),{' '}
      <strong>Z80</strong> (pasmo/pasmonext and sjasmplus, including the Spectrum Next's
      Z80N), <strong>68000</strong> (vasm, with Amiga hunk-executable output),{' '}
      <strong>6809</strong> (lwasm), and <strong>65816</strong> (ca65). The other CPUs
      are checked with instruction-set sweeps, round trips and comparisons against
      reference assemblers. The full list is in the project README on GitHub.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's used</h2>
    <p>
      One binary, one <code>--dialect</code> flag. A few illustrative commands (the full
      dialect and flag set is on GitHub):
    </p>

    {snippets.map((s) => (
      <div class="snippet">
        <div class="snippet-label label">{s.label}</div>
        <pre tabindex="0"><code>{s.code}</code></pre>
      </div>
    ))}

    <h2>Why it's in the family</h2>
    <p>
      It's the tool that assembles Code198x's lesson code — the C64, NES and Spectrum
      assembly curricula all build byte-identical through it, so the code in a lesson is
      exactly what the reference assembler produces. The
      shared <code>isa</code> spec at its core is also the encoding source of truth that
      Emu198x validates its decoders against — one set of facts, three siblings. It's
      its own project with its own roadmap, not a Code198x component.
    </p>
</FamilyExplainerLayout>
